# Design Patterns Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines recommend design patterns appropriate for Flutter development. While not every pattern applies to every situation, understanding and applying these patterns can significantly improve code quality, reusability, and maintainability.

## Best Practices

### Factory Pattern
- Use for flexible object creation without exposing implementation details
- Create objects of varying types from a common interface
- Implement for component creation with varying implementations
- Useful for platform-specific implementations with a common API
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Factory pattern for creating platform-specific widgets
  abstract class PlatformButton {
    Widget build(BuildContext context, String label, VoidCallback onPressed);

    factory PlatformButton() {
      if (Platform.isIOS) {
        return IOSButton();
      } else {
        return AndroidButton();
      }
    }
  }

  class IOSButton implements PlatformButton {
    @override
    Widget build(BuildContext context, String label, VoidCallback onPressed) {
      return CupertinoButton(
        onPressed: onPressed,
        child: Text(label),
      );
    }
  }

  class AndroidButton implements PlatformButton {
    @override
    Widget build(BuildContext context, String label, VoidCallback onPressed) {
      return ElevatedButton(
        onPressed: onPressed,
        child: Text(label),
      );
    }
  }

  // Usage
  Widget buildButton(BuildContext context) {
    final button = PlatformButton();
    return button.build(context, 'Click me', () {
      print('Button clicked');
    });
  }
  ```

### Singleton Pattern
- Use for managing global state or resources
- Implement for services that should have only one instance
- Apply for shared resources like database connections
- Useful for caches, logging services, and app configuration
- Use with caution as it can introduce global state and testing challenges
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Singleton using factory constructor
  class DatabaseService {
    static final DatabaseService _instance = DatabaseService._internal();
    late final Database _database;

    // Factory constructor returns the same instance each time
    factory DatabaseService() {
      return _instance;
    }

    // Private constructor for internal use
    DatabaseService._internal();

    // Initialize the database
    Future<void> initialize() async {
      _database = await openDatabase('app_database.db');
    }

    // Database operations
    Future<List<Map<String, dynamic>>> query(String table) {
      return _database.query(table);
    }
  }

  // Usage
  final dbService = DatabaseService();
  await dbService.initialize();
  final users = await dbService.query('users');

  // Alternative implementation with GetIt dependency injection
  final getIt = GetIt.instance;

  void setupSingletons() {
    getIt.registerLazySingleton<DatabaseService>(() => DatabaseService());
  }
  ```

### Observer Pattern (Provider/Listener)
- Use for state management and propagating changes to UI
- Implement for reactive programming patterns
- Apply when widgets need to respond to data changes
- Useful with state management libraries like Provider, Riverpod, or BLoC
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Simple ChangeNotifier implementation
  class CounterProvider extends ChangeNotifier {
    int _count = 0;

    int get count => _count;

    void increment() {
      _count++;
      notifyListeners();
    }
  }

  // BLoC pattern implementation
  class CounterBloc extends Bloc<CounterEvent, CounterState> {
    CounterBloc() : super(CounterState(0)) {
      on<IncrementEvent>((event, emit) {
        emit(CounterState(state.count + 1));
      });
    }
  }

  // Usage with Provider
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ChangeNotifierProvider(
      create: (_) => CounterProvider(),
      child: Consumer<CounterProvider>(
        builder: (context, provider, child) {
          return Text('Count: ${provider.count}');
        },
      ),
    );
  }

  // Usage with BLoC
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return BlocProvider(
      create: (_) => CounterBloc(),
      child: BlocBuilder<CounterBloc, CounterState>(
        builder: (context, state) {
          return Text('Count: ${state.count}');
        },
      ),
    );
  }
  ```

### Strategy Pattern
- Use for selecting algorithm or behavior at runtime
- Implement for varying implementations of a behavior
- Apply when different algorithms can be interchanged
- Useful for sorting, filtering, or processing data in different ways
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Strategy pattern for payment processing
  abstract class PaymentStrategy {
    Future<bool> processPayment(double amount);
  }

  class CreditCardStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    final String cardNumber;
    final String expiryDate;
    final String cvv;

    CreditCardStrategy(this.cardNumber, this.expiryDate, this.cvv);

    @override
    Future<bool> processPayment(double amount) async {
      // Credit card processing logic
      return true;
    }
  }

  class PayPalStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    final String email;
    final String password;

    PayPalStrategy(this.email, this.password);

    @override
    Future<bool> processPayment(double amount) async {
      // PayPal processing logic
      return true;
    }
  }

  // Context class that uses the strategy
  class PaymentProcessor {
    PaymentStrategy? _strategy;

    void setStrategy(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
      _strategy = strategy;
    }

    Future<bool> makePayment(double amount) {
      if (_strategy == null) {
        throw Exception('Payment strategy not set');
      }
      return _strategy!.processPayment(amount);
    }
  }

  // Usage
  final processor = PaymentProcessor();

  // User selects credit card payment
  processor.setStrategy(CreditCardStrategy('4111111111111111', '12/24', '123'));
  final success = await processor.makePayment(99.99);

  // User switches to PayPal
  processor.setStrategy(PayPalStrategy('user@example.com', 'password'));
  final success2 = await processor.makePayment(59.99);
  ```

### Builder Pattern
- Use for constructing complex objects step by step
- Implement for objects with many optional parameters
- Apply when object construction involves multiple steps
- Useful for creating immutable objects with many properties
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Builder pattern for a complex query
  class QueryBuilder {
    String? _table;
    List<String> _columns = [];
    String? _whereClause;
    List<String> _orderBy = [];
    int? _limit;

    QueryBuilder table(String table) {
      _table = table;
      return this;
    }

    QueryBuilder select(List<String> columns) {
      _columns = columns;
      return this;
    }

    QueryBuilder where(String whereClause) {
      _whereClause = whereClause;
      return this;
    }

    QueryBuilder orderBy(List<String> orderBy) {
      _orderBy = orderBy;
      return this;
    }

    QueryBuilder limit(int limit) {
      _limit = limit;
      return this;
    }

    Query build() {
      if (_table == null) {
        throw Exception('Table name is required');
      }

      return Query(
        table: _table!,
        columns: _columns,
        whereClause: _whereClause,
        orderBy: _orderBy,
        limit: _limit,
      );
    }
  }

  class Query {
    final String table;
    final List<String> columns;
    final String? whereClause;
    final List<String> orderBy;
    final int? limit;

    Query({
      required this.table,
      this.columns = const [],
      this.whereClause,
      this.orderBy = const [],
      this.limit,
    });

    String toSql() {
      // Generate SQL from query parameters
      final columnsClause = columns.isEmpty ? '*' : columns.join(', ');
      var sql = 'SELECT $columnsClause FROM $table';

      if (whereClause != null) {
        sql += ' WHERE $whereClause';
      }

      if (orderBy.isNotEmpty) {
        sql += ' ORDER BY ${orderBy.join(', ')}';
      }

      if (limit != null) {
        sql += ' LIMIT $limit';
      }

      return sql;
    }
  }

  // Usage
  final query = QueryBuilder()
    .table('users')
    .select(['id', 'name', 'email'])
    .where('age > 18')
    .orderBy(['name ASC'])
    .limit(10)
    .build();

  final sql = query.toSql();
  // SQL: "SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE age > 18 ORDER BY name ASC LIMIT 10"
  ```

### Repository Pattern
- Use for abstracting data sources and operations
- Implement for separating domain from data access logic
- Apply when working with multiple data sources
- Useful for switching between local and remote data
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Repository pattern for user data
  abstract class UserRepository {
    Future<User?> getUser(String id);
    Future<List<User>> getAllUsers();
    Future<void> saveUser(User user);
    Future<void> deleteUser(String id);
  }

  class ApiUserRepository implements UserRepository {
    final ApiClient _apiClient;

    ApiUserRepository(this._apiClient);

    @override
    Future<User?> getUser(String id) async {
      final response = await _apiClient.get('/users/$id');
      return User.fromJson(response);
    }

    @override
    Future<List<User>> getAllUsers() async {
      final response = await _apiClient.get('/users');
      return (response as List).map((json) => User.fromJson(json)).toList();
    }

    @override
    Future<void> saveUser(User user) async {
      await _apiClient.post('/users', body: user.toJson());
    }

    @override
    Future<void> deleteUser(String id) async {
      await _apiClient.delete('/users/$id');
    }
  }

  class LocalUserRepository implements UserRepository {
    final Database _db;

    LocalUserRepository(this._db);

    @override
    Future<User?> getUser(String id) async {
      final result = await _db.query('users', where: 'id = ?', whereArgs: [id]);
      if (result.isEmpty) return null;
      return User.fromMap(result.first);
    }

    @override
    Future<List<User>> getAllUsers() async {
      final results = await _db.query('users');
      return results.map((map) => User.fromMap(map)).toList();
    }

    @override
    Future<void> saveUser(User user) async {
      await _db.insert('users', user.toMap(), conflictAlgorithm: ConflictAlgorithm.replace);
    }

    @override
    Future<void> deleteUser(String id) async {
      await _db.delete('users', where: 'id = ?', whereArgs: [id]);
    }
  }

  // Repository factory to choose implementation based on connectivity
  class UserRepositoryFactory {
    final ApiClient _apiClient;
    final Database _database;
    final ConnectivityService _connectivity;

    UserRepositoryFactory(this._apiClient, this._database, this._connectivity);

    UserRepository create() {
      if (_connectivity.isOnline) {
        return ApiUserRepository(_apiClient);
      } else {
        return LocalUserRepository(_database);
      }
    }
  }
  ```

### Adapter Pattern
- Use for making incompatible interfaces work together
- Implement when integrating third-party libraries with different interfaces
- Apply when existing code needs to work with new interfaces
- Examples:
  ```dart
  // Existing interface
  abstract class LocalNotification {
    void show(int id, String title, String body);
    void cancel(int id);
  }

  // Third-party library with a different interface
  class FlutterLocalNotificationsPlugin {
    Future<void> show(
      int id,
      String? title,
      String? body,
      NotificationDetails notificationDetails
    ) async {
      // Implementation
    }

    Future<void> cancel(int id) async {
      // Implementation
    }
  }

  // Adapter to make third-party library work with existing interface
  class FlutterLocalNotificationsAdapter implements LocalNotification {
    final FlutterLocalNotificationsPlugin _plugin;
    final NotificationDetails _defaultDetails;

    FlutterLocalNotificationsAdapter(this._plugin, this._defaultDetails);

    @override
    void show(int id, String title, String body) {
      _plugin.show(id, title, body, _defaultDetails);
    }

    @override
    void cancel(int id) {
      _plugin.cancel(id);
    }
  }
  ```

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- Organize patterns by feature and responsibility
- Place abstract classes/interfaces in separate files
- Group related pattern implementations together
- Create dedicated directories for common patterns:
  ```
  lib/
    core/
      factories/
        widget_factory.dart
      builders/
        form_builder.dart
      repositories/
        base_repository.dart
    data/
      repositories/
        user_repository_impl.dart
      strategies/
        cache_strategies.dart
    domain/
      repositories/
        user_repository.dart
    utils/
      singletons/
        app_config.dart
  ```

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Name classes to clearly indicate the pattern (e.g., `UserBuilder`, `PaymentStrategy`)
- Use consistent naming for related pattern components
- Document pattern intent and usage in class comments
- Follow Flutter/Dart conventions for each pattern
- Prefer composition over inheritance when appropriate
- Keep pattern implementations clean and focused

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Initialize singletons lazily to avoid startup overhead
- Cache factory objects when creation is expensive
- Implement flyweight pattern for shared resource instances
- Optimize builder patterns with default values
- Use factories to create pre-configured instances
- Select appropriate patterns based on performance needs
- Consider memory implications of pattern implementations

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Mock interfaces used in patterns for testability
- Test each strategy implementation independently
- Verify singleton state is properly maintained
- Test builder objects produce correct configurations
- Create test factories for generating test data
- Validate that repositories properly abstract data sources
- Test that patterns work correctly in combination